WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.351
The controlled experiments run on Contextual
Stochastic Block Models,

00:00:04.431 --> 00:00:08.589
which let the authors compute the Bayes optimal
reference exactly.

00:00:08.669 --> 00:00:12.955
Each graph has one thousand nodes and is
parametrized to mimic CORA,

00:00:13.035 --> 00:00:17.385
matching its expected number of same-class and
different-class edges.

00:00:17.465 --> 00:00:21.495
A parameter K controls how discriminative the node
features are,

00:00:21.575 --> 00:00:23.164
swept from zero-point-one,

00:00:23.244 --> 00:00:28.109
where features carry almost no signal and structure
matters most, up to five,

00:00:28.189 --> 00:00:30.420
where structure becomes unnecessary.

00:00:30.500 --> 00:00:35.814
They inductively sample a thousand test nodes per
graph and average over ten graphs.

00:00:35.894 --> 00:00:43.070
Results are corroborated on the real-world Cora-ML
graph and on a Barabási-Albert model with community structure.
